WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.002
-My next guest is the former
mayor of South Bend, Indiana,

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:05.538
a New York Times best-selling
author, and the nominee

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.107
for United States
Secretary of Transportation.

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:10.743
It's my pleasure to welcome back
to the show

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:12.912
Mayor Pete Buttigieg!

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.314
Hey, Mayor Pete,
thank you so much

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:17.450
for being on our show
and coming back.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.619
You're in Washington, D.C.,
right now

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.188
because you attended the --

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.023
President Biden's
inauguration today.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:28.228
You were the first person I saw
this morning to enter almost.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:31.397
How did it feel to be there?

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:32.732
-You know, it was amazing.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:35.168
It was moving.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:38.871
It was reassuring
to be on those Capitol steps

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:40.639
where just two weeks earlier,

00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:42.475
we'd seen those
horrifying images

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.678
of a mob seeking
to take over the Capitol.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.814
What we saw today
was one of the most important

00:00:48.814 --> 00:00:51.451
and oldest rituals
in American democracy,

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.986
that transfer of power.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.187
And I think for a lot of us,

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:57.089
things that maybe
we used to think of as boring

00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:00.593
or take for granted, you know,
on a day like this,

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.995
at a time like this,
actually meant a lot,

00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:05.865
that there was
that -- that continuity.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.100
The president, I thought,
gave a great speech.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.902
There were these
musical performances,

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.472
Garth Brooks, J.Lo, Gaga,

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:15.508
and also this amazing poet,
Amanda Gorman,

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:18.010
who really
set the tone for the day.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.512
So it was a great day.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.180
-She was amazing.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.216
I saw when you first --

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:24.884
You were one of the first people
to get there,

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.987
and I know you probably can't,

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:30.390
being where you are
and your stature,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:35.694
but I couldn't help but get
emotional watching everything.

00:01:35.694 --> 00:01:37.196
Just even everyone just coming,

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.632
the way that the Capitol
was decorated,

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:43.036
it made me so patriotic
and so proud,

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.204
and just seeing the flag flying,

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:48.874
and I go yes. It made me so --
It was like, I felt like

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:52.745
I was, you know, in a George
M. Cohan song or something.

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.748
-Yeah, you know, I didn't used
to care about symbols so much.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.216
especially when I was
first getting involved

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:58.617
in public service
when I was mayor.

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.287
I cared about policy. I cared
about getting things done,

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.055
and events didn't mean
as much to me.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:05.625
But I think just with everything
we've been through as a country,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.526
we realize that
those symbols matters.

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:09.562
The continuity of it matters,

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:13.065
the kind of process
that we go through,

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:16.002
because it reminds us
of the stability of the country.

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.238
It reminds us
what we're trying to protect

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:20.439
when we protect
American democracy.

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:23.208
-What do you think is next
for President Biden

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:27.713
and how he's going
to get the country to unite?

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.282
-Well, I think we saw in his
speech

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.016
that he's calling out
to everybody.

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:33.952
He's making clear he wants to be
a president for everyone,

00:02:33.952 --> 00:02:36.189
whether you voted for him
or not.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.591
But I think in the long run, the
best way to unite the country

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.359
will be to deliver results.

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:42.127
I know that's gonna
be his focus.

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:45.331
The best way I think to bring
people together is to make sure

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.900
that life in this country
gets better,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.301
that our economy is strong,

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:51.104
that there's safety,
that there's justice and equity.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:53.373
And there's a ton of work
starting right away,

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.841
and it really starts of course
with defeating the pandemic.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.778
So it was great that
even before the inauguration,

00:02:59.778 --> 00:03:03.282
as president-elect, he laid out
the first steps of that plan.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.351
Now as president, he's already
working to deliver them,

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:09.054
and I'm excited to be part
of the team to make that happen.

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:11.991
-I don't know if I'll be able
to call you Mayor Pete anymore,

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:15.428
because hopefully,
tomorrow you will be confirmed

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.430
for the role of
Secretary of Transportation.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.933
This is a historic nomination.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:23.502
You'll be the first
Millennial cabinet member,

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:26.438
and you'll also be
the first openly gay person

00:03:26.438 --> 00:03:28.507
to serve in a cabinet position.

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:29.675
What does that all mean to you,

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:32.177
or have you even
thought about it?

00:03:32.177 --> 00:03:33.545
-Yeah, it means a lot.

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:35.815
You know, I can remember
being in high school

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.216
watching on the news

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:40.853
when President Clinton nominated
someone to be an ambassador

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.789
who didn't even get the chance
to get a vote in the Senate

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.090
because he was gay.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.093
And it sent a message.

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:48.727
At that age,
I hadn't really thought

00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:52.265
that I would be serving
in public service like I am now,

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:53.700
but it sent a message, you know,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.270
not only that gay people might
be denied a chance to serve

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.738
but also denied
a chance to belong.

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:02.475
So the fact
that 20-some years later,

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.076
tomorrow, I'll go
before a committee

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:07.614
and hopefully receive a vote
that will make it possible

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:09.415
once the confirmation
vote comes up

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.617
to become a confirmed
cabinet member --

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.453
I just hope that that
sends a different, better,

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:18.057
opposite message to some kid
who's watching right now,

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:22.194
that you have every right
to belong, every right to serve,

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:25.431
every right
to be part of this country.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.333
-Four years from now,
what do you hope

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:30.603
that you will have accomplished
in the role?

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.171
-You know, I'd love to be able
to look back

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.707
on a tenure as secretary
and say first of all

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:40.746
that travel was and remains safe
for workers and for travelers,

00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:42.014
but also above and beyond that,

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:44.950
that America restored
its leadership role

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.885
when it came to infrastructure.

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:50.323
We've fallen behind.
We've been expected to tolerate

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:53.526
roads and bridges
falling into disrepair,

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:57.563
passenger rail being way behind
what our fellow --

00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.366
our, you know, counterparts,
citizens in other countries

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:01.901
are able to enjoy,

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.203
just a lot of things
where we've settled for less,

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:06.272
and I think now is a chance
to do something about that.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:08.274
I'd love to look back
on these years and say

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:12.412
this was when electric vehicles
became much more widely adopted

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:14.080
and the possibilities there

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.181
with technology
around electric vehicles,

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:19.051
around automated vehicles if we
can get the safety part right,

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:21.053
and a whole bunch of other
things that are coming

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:24.823
could allow us to say that
America became better prepared

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:27.160
for the climate crisis,
more just,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.562
and more economically sound

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:33.299
because we did what we had to do
when it came to infrastructure.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:36.134
I know it's seen as
kind of a nerdy topic sometimes.

00:05:36.134 --> 00:05:38.504
But I think it's one of
the most interesting things

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:41.474
that is before
the administration right now,

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:43.509
and I'm really excited
to do my part.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.112
-Good luck tomorrow, and
anything we can do to help --

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:48.781
We'd love to have you
back on the show whenever.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:51.083
Thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:52.418
Mayor Pete Buttigieg.

